WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.276 --> 00:00:10.444
Logos are everywhere.

00:00:11.178 --> 00:00:12.946
They're a huge part of daily life,

00:00:13.747 --> 00:00:15.482
from the toothpaste
we use in the morning,

00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:17.384
the insoles of our shoes,

00:00:17.584 --> 00:00:18.752
cereal packaging,

00:00:18.919 --> 00:00:19.753
phone apps -

00:00:20.354 --> 00:00:23.323
most of the products we see and use
are branded with some form

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.025
of symbol or wordmark.

00:00:25.526 --> 00:00:26.393
Why is that?

00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:30.497
Logos aren't just a label
that shows who's responsible

00:00:30.564 --> 00:00:31.798
for a product or service,

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:33.600
they're also used because

00:00:33.667 --> 00:00:36.770
people often choose products
based on their perceived value

00:00:36.937 --> 00:00:38.605
rather than their actual value.

00:00:39.573 --> 00:00:42.743
Think of the celebrity who drives
an Aston Martin instead of say,

00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:43.877
a Skoda.

00:00:43.944 --> 00:00:47.481
Skoda has often been ranked Car of
the Year in many European countries

00:00:47.814 --> 00:00:50.551
and delivers much better mileage
at one tenth of the price.

00:00:51.518 --> 00:00:53.220
It's the logical choice.

00:00:53.287 --> 00:00:57.724
But it's Aston Martin's identity that
creates images of luxury and status

00:00:57.791 --> 00:01:00.027
and that is what usually
clinches the sale.

00:01:01.228 --> 00:01:02.996
A logo can appear in various formats

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.464
depending on both the brand

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.733
and the environment
in which the logo is displayed.

00:01:07.301 --> 00:01:08.802
Wordmarks are type-based logos

00:01:08.869 --> 00:01:11.138
that draw attention to
a brand name on its own.

00:01:11.605 --> 00:01:13.407
The type design often
gets a custom twist

00:01:13.473 --> 00:01:15.776
to capture the essence
of what the brand does

00:01:16.476 --> 00:01:18.278
and to separate it from competitors.

00:01:18.946 --> 00:01:22.082
That could be the loose, flowing feel
of Cadbury's or Coca Cola

00:01:22.149 --> 00:01:24.718
or the more traditional vibe
of The New York Times

00:01:24.785 --> 00:01:26.253
and The Washington Post.

00:01:27.254 --> 00:01:29.990
Visual tricks such as
the use of negative space

00:01:30.057 --> 00:01:32.292
can be applied to add
little design magic.

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:37.164
An example is the hidden FedEx arrow
that conveys direction and movement.

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.633
Monograms are shortened wordmarks

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.568
that are usually initials
of longer names.

00:01:42.603 --> 00:01:44.271
These are about turning
a mouthful into something

00:01:44.338 --> 00:01:46.273
that's generally easier to remember.

00:01:48.342 --> 00:01:51.144
Another form a logo can take
is that of a graphic symbol.

00:01:51.578 --> 00:01:53.914
Still representing the core
of a brand's service,

00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:57.217
it's often used where space
is at a premium -

00:01:57.684 --> 00:01:58.819
like an avatar,

00:01:58.886 --> 00:02:01.488
or that little area
beside a website URL.

00:02:02.422 --> 00:02:04.992
Sometimes when a brand
is so established

00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:07.361
and the symbol is
instantly recognisable

00:02:07.661 --> 00:02:09.763
anything else
becomes almost unnecessary.

00:02:10.664 --> 00:02:13.934
Symbols are particularly well suited
for incorporating subtle messages

00:02:14.001 --> 00:02:16.803
that act as talking points
and heighten memorability.

00:02:17.604 --> 00:02:20.274
The bear in the Toblerone logo
is one example -

00:02:22.009 --> 00:02:25.145
it adds meaning because the bear
relates to an old coat of arms

00:02:25.212 --> 00:02:27.247
that represents the town
where the Toblerone chocolate

00:02:27.314 --> 00:02:28.649
was originally created.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.019
The main job of a logo
is to identify.

00:02:32.352 --> 00:02:35.088
It takes its meaning
from the entity it represents

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:36.557
not the other way around.

00:02:37.291 --> 00:02:40.060
This is why, when logos
first enter the world,

00:02:40.127 --> 00:02:42.095
they're essentially empty vessels.

00:02:42.829 --> 00:02:44.598
With consistent use over time

00:02:44.665 --> 00:02:47.434
the mark gets filled with every
association people have

00:02:47.501 --> 00:02:48.936
with a particular brand,

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:52.272
whether that's positive, negative
or something in between.

00:02:52.606 --> 00:02:54.808
That's exactly how
religious symbols work.

00:02:55.509 --> 00:02:57.244
There's nothing inherent
in the shapes,

00:02:57.711 --> 00:02:59.880
but it's about what those shapes
have come to represent

00:02:59.947 --> 00:03:01.915
in the minds of the people
who are looking at them.

00:03:03.116 --> 00:03:05.719
Another form of logo
is that of a combination mark -

00:03:05.986 --> 00:03:08.522
essentially a wordmark
and symbol together.

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:11.925
This lets you communicate messages
in a slightly different way.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:15.729
Look at how the Amazon logo
has it all from A to Z

00:03:15.996 --> 00:03:18.265
and even adds a little smile
into the mix.

00:03:18.332 --> 00:03:20.367
So how can you tell
a good logo from bad?

00:03:21.902 --> 00:03:23.337
In my work as a designer

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:26.240
I judge the strength of an idea
on the following criteria -

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:34.481
By appropriate I mean
the design needs to be relevant

00:03:34.548 --> 00:03:36.650
in shape and form
to the client in question.

00:03:37.084 --> 00:03:40.287
For example, a logo for a toy maker
may need to be fun

00:03:40.354 --> 00:03:41.522
and appealing to kids

00:03:42.256 --> 00:03:45.692
or a logo for a car racing team
may need to appear sleek and dynamic.

00:03:47.361 --> 00:03:50.998
By simple I mean that the design
can't say too much.

00:03:51.765 --> 00:03:52.900
It should tell one story

00:03:52.966 --> 00:03:55.836
so it's adaptable enough
for use in all applications

00:03:56.136 --> 00:03:58.305
from on a billboard
or the side of the building

00:03:58.372 --> 00:04:01.175
to a social media avatar
or a website favicon.

00:04:01.675 --> 00:04:05.212
By memorable I mean that the design
also needs to be distinctive -

00:04:05.779 --> 00:04:07.548
unusual enough to be remembered.

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:11.885
A good way of testing this is to
describe the design to someone

00:04:11.952 --> 00:04:14.288
and see if they can sketch
a fairly accurate likeness.

00:04:15.589 --> 00:04:18.592
Some of my favourite logos
aren't particularly well-known

00:04:18.892 --> 00:04:21.862
but they've stuck with me because
they all share those same traits.

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:25.999
Telling a relevant story
with simplicity and distinction.

